re are companies that biting by  those those print those jurisdictions  laws  can set up shell companies can set up  trusts that are almost impenetrable  where you can hide assets it's how they  end up being used that is usually the  problem you can hide illegal gains you  can hide proceeds from corruption you  can hide assets from creditors  uh or even family members and it's  almost impossible to get to and that's  what that's a big part of the problem  you know all the numbers show that most  people in this country a don't have a  savings account  be or are thinking this is this is a  rich person's problem or rich person's  game rather so if i'm sitting at home  and i'm watching you and i'm trying to  just make ends meet living paycheck to  paycheck why should i care about this  how does this affect me  i think it affects ordinary people in  many many ways it takes away tax  revenues that would otherwise help to  pay for services in the in the countries  where we live including in the united  states uh it uh it actually  enables many categories of crime from  drug trafficking to political corruption  it makes it harder for us to hold  politicians accountable when politicians  skirt the law  and and uh engage in corruption and  pocket uh payments they have ways to  hide it that make it very difficult to  find and that undermines our democracy  that undermines good governance so it  affects us all in many many ways  greg this has been described as a  parallel financial universe with  corrosive effects that could span  generations how so  well it contributes to a lot of problems  that are really endemic and that are  growing right now including you know  wealth disparities in this in the united  states and in other countries when when  the wealthiest have the ability to hide  much of their money in jurisdictions  where they're not paying the same tax  rates they would otherwise that helps  them hold on to that wealth and grow  that wealth whereas those who are less  fortunate are paying their share if not  more and making up for that difference  so it contributes to a lot of issues  that that actually get worse the longer  that this persists  what's the solution greg  well it's very difficult i mean the the  united states many we've had president  after president condemn  uh the offshore system but even in the  united states as we report in the post  there are states including south dakota  nevada alaska that are increasingly  passing  financial secrecy laws of their own that  and  effectively function as offshore havens  within the united states uh part of the  problem is that this is this is a  banking system or a financial system  that takes advantage of the fact that  money moves past national borders so  it's hard for any country or even a  group of countries to gain much headway  yeah there were a lot of well-known  names on this list but there were no  americans  no that's not true there are some  americans but not the most wealthy  americans not jeff bezos for example  or mark zuckerberg or facebook they  don't show up in the documents that  could be because these documents came  from financial services companies in 14  places around the world  that america's wealthiest people just  don't use or perhaps american  billionaires are doing other things with  their money besides putting them in  shell companies or trusts in these  offshore systems that we're writing  about  all right thank you greg as you said  it's not uh it's not illegal but the  optics sure don't look good or feel good  to be honest with you thank you  thank you very much for your time this  morning thanks
 

